# The closed set of computed-value kinds, and the classification of
# every computed-value text @webref/css carries at the pinned version.
#
# A **kind** says which conversion a property needs to take a specified
# value to a computed one -- not the value's *type*, which is
# per-property and which the per-property parsers of ST-M1 will carry.
# The specifications write the computed value as prose, in 169 distinct
# forms at this pin, and that prose is what is classified here, once per
# form rather than once per property. The style spec records 168 forms;
# this pin has 169, which is the kind of movement the pin exists to make
# visible.
#
# The classification is exhaustive in both directions. A form webref
# carries and nothing here classifies leaves a property with no
# conversion, and a classification of a form webref no longer carries is
# a rule that has quietly stopped applying to anything. hg-cssgen fails
# on either.
#
# Where the prose cannot be carried by a table at all -- `display`,
# `font-weight`, the track-sizing properties, the four `overflow-*` --
# the kind is `prose` and the property names a hand-written hook. That
# is the honest place for them: twenty properties with a named function
# each, rather than a classification that pretends to cover them.

kind shorthand "No computed value of its own: the cascade stores the longhands it expands to."
kind identity  "The computed value is the specified value, unchanged."
kind length    "Lengths are made absolute; percentages and keywords pass through."
kind number    "Resolved to a number, clamped where the specification states a range."
kind angle     "Angles are normalized to a canonical unit."
kind time      "Times are resolved to a canonical duration."
kind frequency "Resolved to an absolute frequency."
kind color     "Colors are resolved; everything else passes through."
kind image     "`<image>` values are computed; everything else passes through."
kind url       "`<url>` values are made absolute; everything else passes through."
kind mixed     "More than one of the conversions above applies inside one value, so the conversion walks it."
kind prose     "The specification defines the computed value in prose a table cannot carry; a named hook computes it."

# --- shorthand (2 of 169) -------------------------------------

# 4: -webkit-text-stroke, border-image, mask-border, and 1 more
computed "See individual properties" => shorthand

# 152: -webkit-line-clamp, all, animation, and 149 more
computed "see individual properties" => shorthand

